\par . The V10 Normalization Engine (schema_v10.py) 
I implemented the exact deterministic normalization rules you specified. It guarantees that every single artifact passing through the system is strictly conformed to the V10 schema, while simultaneously keeping a perfect forensic record of the original ra
w JSON payload. The logic for deterministic synthetic IDs (TICKET-SYNTH-xxx, DEC-SYNTH-xxx) and heuristic decision extraction is locked in. 2. The API Server Cutover (api_server.py) 
The archive_ticket and archive_csm endpoints have been stripped of any mock or legacy database routing. Incoming payloads are now instantly piped through schema_v10.py. The strict Dual-Archive payload (containing both normalized and raw keys) is pushed to
 the blockchain via ExtrinsicSubmitter. Note: I kept the Antigravity artifact mirroring intact! So when Substrate receives a ticket, a .md copy still lands instantly in our workspace for UI visualization. 3. The Recovery Scripts 
I wrote recover_tickets.py and armed it with aggressive regex extractors to scrape the 136 transcript_full.jsonl files in your .gemini/antigravity/brain directory. WARNING 
The initial recovery run returned 0 unique artifacts. This implies that the raw JSON representations of tickets/CSMs were not explicitly dumped into the text of the logs that survived the crash. However, the recovered_tickets.json pipeline is ready. If yo
u have any JSON dumps stored elsewhere (like in dashboard/), you can simply drop them into recovered_tickets.json and hit them with python migrate_to_substrate.py. 4. The Blockchain Migrator (migrate_to_substrate.py) 
The migrator is wired up to ingest recovered_tickets.json. It connects directly to ws://127.0.0.1:9944. It performs a pre-flight check to guarantee that it is actually talking to a live Substrate node, and actively aborts the migration if it attempts to f
all back to MOCK mode, preventing any false assurance of data safety. Next Steps Phase 1: Live Telemetry Overlays (Block Lens Backend) 
As part of the Live Telemetry initiative, I have constructed a "Block Lens" backend explorer directly into the API server. This turns the on-chain tickets into viewable portals into the Substrate chain context. 1. Substrate RPC Adapter (explorer/substrate
_adapter.py) Provides deterministic wrapper functions (get_block_by_hash, get_chain_head, get_events, etc.) over the substrateinterface library. Built to never throw exceptions on RPC failures, ensuring the UI degrades gracefully. 
2. Explorer Engine (explorer/block_lens.py, extrinsic_lens.py, ticket_lens.py) Generates structured contextual payloads. The Ticket Lens seamlessly meshes a normalized V10 ticket with its corresponding Substrate block and extrinsic execution summary, surf
acing events (like ArchiveModule.TicketArchived) directly to the client. 3. API Integration (api_server.py) Added three new FastAPI endpoints: GET /explorer/block_lens GET /explorer/extrinsic_lens GET /explorer/ticket_lens/\{ticket_id\} 
The backend is fully primed to serve contextual chain state. Phase 3: Android MCP Server (Remote Operator Control Plane) I have successfully embedded the native Model Context Protocol (MCP) server inside the Android client, transforming the Architect Port
al into a sovereign remote operator capability surface for Gemini. 1. The MCP Configuration (mcp_tools.json) 
I embedded the exact MCP tool definition JSON payload securely in the Android application's assets/ folder. This ensures the available capability boundaries are deterministic and statically packaged with the client. 2. Ktor Embedded Server (McpServer.kt) 
I scaffolded a lightweight Ktor HTTP server (io.ktor:ktor-server-netty) directly inside the Android app listening on port 8080. It exposes two core endpoints: POST /mcp/tools: Serves the JSON definitions to Gemini. 
POST /mcp/execute: Routes tool execution requests to the internal Kotlin dispatcher. 3. Strictly Scoped Handlers I built out the ToolDispatcher and the 4 domain-specific capability handlers: MeshTools: Proxies requests for Tickets, Lenses, and Chain Head 
status, and intercepts ticket archiving to enforce explicit user confirmation before signing. DeviceTools: Reads Android's BatteryManager and LocationManager to provide read-only physical telemetry. 
UiTools: Uses Android's NotificationManager and App Broadcasts to push information and UI navigation events directly to the user's screen. SensorTools: Implements a single-frame camera capture trigger, ensuring Gemini has eyes when requested, without ever
 granting open streaming access. 4. The Foreground Service (McpForegroundService.kt) 
To guarantee the MCP server remains alive even when the app is backgrounded, it is hosted inside an Android Foreground Service. The user is explicitly notified via a persistent "Gemini Remote Operator" system notification while the server is active, provi
ding total transparency. 5. Start/Stop Toggle I wired a simple "}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af44 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\insrsid7026199\charrsid7026199 \u-10178\'3f\u-8938\'3f}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7026199\charrsid7026199 
" toggle directly into the Top App Bar of the MainActivity.kt allowing you to instantly boot up or shut down the MCP Foreground Service. Phase 4: Closed-Loop Self-Testing Organism 
Antigravity can now autonomously drive and test the physical Android device. This decouples the agent from local machine constraints and allows it to fully orchestrate scenarios over a network path, generating automatic Substrate tickets upon failure. 1. 
Automation Tools Exposed (Android) ui_walkthrough_step: Exposes semantic UI actions (click, type, scroll, wait_for, assert) driven by composable selectors (text, ID, xpath, compose tags). 
ui_get_state: Exposes the full screen hierarchy, Compose semantics tree, and focused elements to allow the agent to orient itself. Updated McpModels.kt, mcp_tools.json, and ToolDispatcher.kt to serialize and route these requests to UiAutomationTools.kt an
d UiStateTools.kt. 2. Antigravity Test Runner (Python) Constructed the Scenario Engine in mgsh_mcp/test_runner.py. Features an McpClient to issue remote HTTP instructions to the Android MCP Server. 
Provides declarative Scenario and Step dataclasses to describe user flows effortlessly. V10 Substrate Integration: The FailureReporter natively catches broken expectations or timeouts and automatically POSTs a fully formed V10 Ticket to the backend, prese
rving the step-by-step metadata for root cause analysis. Phase 2 (Cont.): Jetpack Compose Block Lens UI 
I've integrated the visual Block Lens interface directly into the Android application via Jetpack Compose. This fulfills the Phase 2 UX vision of providing real-time cognitive telemetry overlays. BlockLens.kt: Created the core UI scaffolding with componen
ts like BlockLensRoot, BlockSummaryCard, ChainHeadMiniPanel, ExtrinsicsList, EventsList, and TicketsInBlockList. 
All Compose nodes are rigorously tagged with testTag (e.g., "block_lens_root") to ensure that the Antigravity test runner can cleanly assert on their state using the ui_get_state capability. Real-Time Chain Head Ticker ChainHeadTicker.kt: Built a ChainHea
dTickerOverlay and a corresponding ChainHeadTickerViewModel that continuously polls for the latest block head (bestNumber), finalized head, and sync status. Designed as a top-level overlay to be anchored above the ticket view. 
Provides immediate visual cues of the chain's health (e.g., displaying yellow when syncing, green when healthy). Native support for MCP runner assertions via the "chain_head_ticker_overlay" test tag. Phase 5: End-to-End Block Lens Network Wiring The Block
 Lens UX is now fully connected to the live Substrate backend, proving the end-to-end "circulatory system" of the organism. 1. Repository and ViewModel Layer Wired BlockLensRepositoryImpl and BlockLensViewModel to interface directly with the ApiClient. 
Integrated Retrofit and OkHttp Interceptors to handle Android-to-LAN communication securely. Enforced auto-loading on the Block Lens tab, dynamically defaulting to the current chain head on initialization. 2. Destruction of Mocks Completely removed the fa
llback mock dictionaries inside substrate_adapter.py. The system no longer synthesizes #0 blocks when the node goes down; it natively propagates real HTTP 500 exceptions down to the Android client, ensuring strict data integrity and fail-safe UX behaviors
. 
